'''Ōtaki''' (previously '''Otaki''') is a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Ōtaki is [[Tim Costley]] of the [[New Zealand National Party]]. He has held this position since the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]].

The electorate will be abolished ahead of the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
The Ōtaki electorate is a coastal electorate in the lower North Island, centred on the southern [[Horowhenua District|Horowhenua district]] and northern [[Kāpiti Coast District|Kāpiti Coast district]]. Its northern boundary near [[Foxton, New Zealand|Foxton]] and [[Levin, New Zealand|Levin]] is with the [[Rangitīkei (electorate)|Rangitīkei electorate]]. On the east it joins the [[Wairarapa (electorate)|Wairarapa electorate]] in the [[Tararua Range]]. The southern boundary is with [[Mana (electorate)|Mana]] (bisecting [[Paraparaumu]]) and [[Remutaka (electorate)|Remutaka]].

Population centres within the electorate include the towns of [[Ōtaki, New Zealand|Ōtaki]], [[Waikanae]], [[Waitarere]], [[Manakau]] and [[Peka Peka]]. 

==History==

=== 1892 to 1972 ===
In the 1892 electoral redistribution, population shift to the [[North Island]] required the transfer of one seat from the [[South Island]] to the north. The resulting ripple effect saw every electorate established in 1890 have its boundaries altered, and eight electorates were established for the first time, including Otaki.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=59f}} Otaki was first contested in the [[1893 New Zealand general election|1893 election]], and the first member for Otaki was [[James Wilson (New Zealand politician, born 1849)|James Wilson]], who held the seat for one term until 1896. It was then won by [[Henry Augustus Field]] and then, after Henry's death, by his brother [[William Hughes Field]]. William Field, a [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal]]-turn-[[Independent politician|independent]]-turn-[[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform]], held it for a total of 32 years, from 1900 to 1935. Field lost the electorate in [[1911 New Zealand general election|1911]] to [[John Robertson (New Zealand politician, born 1875)|John Robertson]] of the [[Social Democratic Party (New Zealand)|Social Democratic Party]] (who had been nominated by the flax-workers union), but won it back in [[1914 New Zealand general election|1914]].

The seat was abolished in [[1972 New Zealand general election|1972]], and [[Allan McCready]], who was the incumbent, instead stood for and won the [[Manawatu (New Zealand electorate)|Manawatu]] electorate.

=== 1996 to 2026 ===
Otaki was recreated ahead of the change to [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]], by combining two [[Bellwether (politics)|bellwether]] seats: the northern half of [[Kapiti (New Zealand electorate)|Kapiti]] with the entire [[Horowhenua (New Zealand electorate)|Horowhenua]] seat. Its boundaries have been largely unchanged by boundary reviews. The 2007 boundary review revised the electorate's spelling (Ōtaki, with a leading macron). The 2014 boundary review transferred [[Shannon, New Zealand|Shannon]] and [[Tokomaru, New Zealand|Tokomaru]] to [[Rangitīkei (electorate)|Rangitīkei]]. No boundary changes occurred in the 2020 redistribution.<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/%C5%8Dtaki-electorate-profile/ |title=Ōtaki - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 23 September 2025}}</ref>

The first MP for Otaki since its recreation was [[Judy Keall]]. She was succeeded in [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002]] by [[Darren Hughes]], her former executive assistant who became the youngest member of the House of Representatives. Former [[Horowhenua District|Horowhenua district]] councillor [[Nathan Guy]] won in [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008]] on his second attempt and held the electorate until his retirement in 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Counsell |first1=Gerard |title=Swing seats: All eyes on Otaki |url= http://tvnz.co.nz/content/2250215/484445/article.html |access-date=8 October 2014 |work=[[1News|One News]] |date=31 October 2008}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/rural/2019/07/national-party-reshuffle-after-nathan-guy-announces-retirement-from-politics.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190730090508/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/rural/2019/07/national-party-reshuffle-after-nathan-guy-announces-retirement-from-politics.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=30 July 2019|title=National party reshuffle after Nathan Guy announces retirement from politics|work=[[Newshub]] |access-date=2019-11-25|language=en}}</ref> The electorate was held by both Labour and National MPs in its final two terms.

The 2025 boundary review required a reduction in North Island electorates by one to reflect nationwide population shifts. The three Wellington City electorates ([[Rongotai (electorate)|Rongotai]], [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central]] and [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]) were all underpopulated. All were abolished with flow-on effects for the wider region. The [[Mana (electorate)|Mana]] and Ōtaki electorates were also abolished, with the population of Ōtaki being redistributed to [[Kapiti (electorate)|Kapiti]] and [[Rangitīkei (electorate)|Rangitīkei]].

